      'FINRA Registration Rules Registered Representatives',
      'Broker-dealers are generally required to register with the Financial Industry Regulatory Authority (FINRA), and any broker-dealer employees who accept or solicit customer orders must be registered with FINRA. The following broker-dealer personnel must also be registered:\n\n• Operating officers (but not officers who are passive owners)\n• Equity traders\n• Operations (back office) supervisors\n• Individuals involved in investment banking activities\n\nIndividuals responsible for submitting financial reports to self-regulatory organizations (SROs) and the\n\nSecurities and Exchange Commission (SEC)\n\nIndividuals who supervise or train registered persons Note that no registration is required for someone who performs clerical duties.\n\nSIE and Representative Exams\n\nFor an individual to be registered, they must pass the appropriate exams. The first exam is the Securities Industries Essentials (SIE) exam. This exam does not require broker-dealer sponsorship and can be taken by anyone. However, the SIE is merely a prerequisite for registration and does not by itself enable individuals to function in any capacity that requires registration.\n\nOnce an individual passes the SIE, the results will remain valid for four years. If an individual does not associate with a firm within four years, they will be required to retake the SIE. In order to become a registered representative, an individual must be sponsored by a broker-dealer firm and pass the SIE, as well as the appropriate representative qualification exam (e.g., Series 6 or Series 7). The application for registration is called a U4 form. The U4 form is filed with FINRA by a broker-dealer on behalf of their employees. Form U4 contains detailed personal information, including:\n\n• Physical characteristics\n\n10-year employment history\n\n• Five-year residence history\n• Outside business activities\n• Criminal history\n• Civil judicial proceedings\n\nBankruptcy judgments, liens, and compromises with creditors (renegotiating the amount of a debt)\n\n• Customer complaints\n• Prior terminations due to improper behavior\n• Attestation that the person has been or will soon be fingerprinted\n• Any compromises with creditors\n\nU4 amendment: Whenever any of this information changes (e.g., a change of residence, or a new customer complaint), the employee and the member firm must amend Form U4 within 30 days. When disciplinary events such as a criminal conviction or making false statements on an application may lead to a statutory disqualification from membership, Form U4 must be amended within 10 days.\n\ny\n\nTest-Taking Tip: Settling credit card debt for a lower amount would need to be reported on the U4. Reportable events: FINRA requires that it be notified in writing promptly, but no later than 30 calendar days, after the member firm knows or should have known if the firm itself or any registered employee is:\n\n• Found to have violated any provision of any securities law\n• The subject of a written customer complaint alleging theft, misappropriation of funds, or forgery\n• Named as a defendant or respondent in any legal proceeding alleging violations of the Securities Acts\n\nbrought by a regulatory authority\n\n• Denied registration or is expelled, suspended, or disciplined by any self-regulatory organization\n• Indicted, convicted, or pleads guilty to any criminal offense (except for misdemeanor traffic violations and\n\nmisdemeanor DUls, but note that criminal DUIs must be reported)\n\n• Associated with any broker-dealer, investment company, investment adviser, or insurance company that was\n\nsuspended, expelled, or had its registration denied, or that was convicted of, or pleaded no contest to, any felony or misdemeanor\n\n• A defendant or respondent in any securities- or commodities-related civil litigation or arbitration settled for\n\nan amount exceeding $15,000 ($25,000 if the settlement involved that member firm itself)\n\nSubject to statutory disqualification\n\nThe subject of disciplinary actions by their firm involving suspension, termination, withholding of commissions, or imposition of fines in excess of $2,500\n\n• Any compromises with creditors\n\nYou may see these referred to as reportable events. Many reportable events also require updating the information on an associated person\'s Form U4. For example, if an associated person is indicted, this is both a reportable event and a required update to the criminal history section of the person\'s Form U4. Both the report and the U4 update can be done at the same time through FINRA Gateway. FINRA Gateway: All of the U4 information is entered into the FINRA Gateway database run by FINRA. Portions of this data are available to the public via a FINRA website called BrokerCheck. FINRA BrokerCheck: FINRA maintains a website called BrokerCheck where the general public can get information on a rep or member firm. The following information is disclosed on BrokerCheck: •Employment history: 10-year employment history, both in and outside the securities industry, taken from the ua Registrations: Securities licenses and registration(s)\n\n• Complaint and disciplinary record: Disciplinary record, which includes both complaints and serious\n\nallegations that have not yet been resolved, as well as any disciplinary or settlement actions taken\n\n• Outside business activities: Any business activity disclosed on Form U4\n\nSpecifically excluded from BrokerCheck is information on residence history, Social Security number, and physical characteristics. BrokerCheck includes information on both representatives who are currently working in the securities industry and representatives who have left the business. It also provides information on each member firm, including its disciplinary record. BrokerCheck also includes previously registered member firms that have shut their doors.\n\nInvestor education: Once every calendar year, member firms must provide the following to each of their customers, in writing:\n\nThe FINRA BrokerCheck hotline number\n\n• A statement describing the availability of an investor brochure that describes FINRA BrokerCheck\n• The FINRA website address',
