      'Continuing Education Requirements',
      'FINRA requires continuing education (CE) for all registered persons. It is broken into two components: the regulatory element and the firm element.\n\n• Regulatory element: Requires that a FINRA-created computerized training session be completed annually. If\n\nthe regulatory element is not completed within the required time period, that person\'s registration is inactive until itis completed. During the inactivity, this person cannot perform any of the functions of a registered representative and cannot be paid.\n\nFirm element: Requires that an annual training plan be prepared and implemented by member firms to cover relevant products, regulations, and compliance issues.\n\nLeaving and Rejoining the Securities Industry\n\nIfa registered person leaves their firm, a Form US must be filed with FINRA within 30 days of termination. In addition, a copy of the US must be supplied to the terminated individual.\n\nNote: The word "termination" here doesn\'t just mean getting fired. Form U5 must also be filed when a\n\nregistered person retires, moves to a new firm, or leaves their job for any reason. The person\'s FINRA registration is terminated at the same time as their employment. The person\'s registration is not reinstated until another firm registers that individual through a new U4. FINRA prohibits firms from parking (a.k.a. hanging) an individual\'s registration. This is when a firm continues to indicate that an individual is a registered person with the firm, when the individual is not actually doing work for the firm. FINRA maintains jurisdiction over an associated person for two years after a Form US is processed. This means that the person can still be subject to FINRA disciplinary proceedings or arbitration for the person\'s actions before leaving the industry.',
